WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
back to "Get Cooking

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.600
in Cloud," where we
share the best recipes

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.370
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.160
I'm Priyanka, and
today is our final day

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.067
in our gluten-free kitchen
with our special guest, Jenny.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.150
JENNY BROWN: That's right.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.160
We've been through so
much on this journey,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.800
from realizing it was
more than just a recipe

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.530
to making sure allergens
and personally identifiable

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.000
information stay
where they belong.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.070
So let's jump back in.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:34.600
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: In
the last two episodes,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.225
we've talked about
Dinner Winner, which

00:00:36.225 --> 00:00:38.740
is an application that
collects recipes from users

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.630
all over the world,
and the winning recipe

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.660
makes it on their platform
and receives an award.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.510
We know there might be personal
information initially tied

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.600
to these submissions that need
to be removed before anything

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.930
else can happen.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.800
And we want this to be a
seamless automated process.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.890
We saw an architecture of how
they can automate and classify

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.970
the uploaded data by using Cloud
Storage, Cloud Functions, DLP,

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:01.908
and pub/sub.

00:01:01.908 --> 00:01:03.700
JENNY BROWN: We created
a four-step process

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.580
for the Dinner Winner team to
get this solution implemented.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.320
And we were halfway
there by setting up

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.440
our project and our permissions
and creating the Google Cloud

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:12.657
Storage bucket and pub/sub.

00:01:12.657 --> 00:01:14.740
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Today,
we combine the remaining

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.662
ingredients and bake.

00:01:15.662 --> 00:01:17.620
JENNY BROWN: When making
something gluten-free,

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.030
we need to check on two things--

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.370
our ingredients are
gluten-free and our environment

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.390
is gluten-free.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.348
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Similarly for Dinner Winner,

00:01:24.348 --> 00:01:25.810
we need two Cloud
Functions-- one

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.720
that is invoked when an object
is uploaded to Google Cloud

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.850
Storage and the
other that is invoked

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.180
when a message is received
in the Cloud pub/sub queue.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.730
Let's start by creating the
Cloud Function that triggers

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.530
from the Cloud Storage bucket.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.260
We will open Cloud Functions
and create a new function.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.800
Name it as Create_DLP_job.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.290
In the trigger field, we
will select Cloud Storage

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.350
and select the quarantine
bucket from the bucket browser.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.430
Select Python 3.7
for the runtime.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.422
And in the inline
editor, copy and paste

00:01:55.422 --> 00:01:57.130
the code from the link
in the description

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.060
below in main.py and
requirements.txt.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.230
You will find that there is a
Create_DLP_job function, which

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:07.820
creates the DLP job
from when a recipe is

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.590
uploaded in the bucket.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.950
So in the function
to execute, we

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.700
will replace the hello_GCS
with Create_DLP_job

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:16.742
and create that function.

00:02:16.742 --> 00:02:19.200
JENNY BROWN: Now, we need to
create the Cloud Function that

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.850
gets invoked when a message is
received in the Cloud pub/sub

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:22.622
queue.

00:02:22.622 --> 00:02:24.580
It's pretty much the
same, but this time, we'll

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.070
choose Cloud pub/sub
in the trigger field

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:29.170
and enter our pub/sub topic.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.640
We will use the same
function code as last time,

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.830
but in the function to execute,
we use resolve_DLP this time.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.830
And with that, we have both
our Cloud Functions ready.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:40.720
And now for the moment of truth.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.390
Has our recipe for secure
application automation

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.170
done the trick
for Dinner Winner?

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.003
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I
think we've effectively

00:02:47.003 --> 00:02:49.460
avoided any personal
information cross-contamination,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.597
but we will need to test
it out and make sure.

00:02:51.597 --> 00:02:53.680
JENNY BROWN: So we've got
some sample recipes that

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.690
have some sensitive
data in them and some

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.280
that have non-sensitive data.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.488
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: We are
defining the sensitive data

00:02:59.488 --> 00:03:01.845
as name, email address,
location, and phone numbers,

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:03.220
but you can change
it in the code

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.610
to define it values or
info types that you prefer.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.200
JENNY BROWN: OK,
so let's test it.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.200
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: We
will do it in Cloud Shell.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.210
Clone the git repo,
navigate to the sample data,

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.900
and then copy the files over
to GCS quarantine bucket.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.660
The DLP API inspects
and classifies

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.330
each file uploaded to
the quarantine bucket

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.210
and moves it to the
appropriate target bucket based

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:26.452
on its classification.

00:03:26.452 --> 00:03:27.910
JENNY BROWN: Let's
open our buckets

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.100
and review the uploaded files.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.680
Let's click on the quarantine
bucket and click on a file

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.590
and see that it does not
have any sensitive data.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.300
Let's also check
out one of the files

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.860
from the non-sensitive bucket.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.110
And we see that it does not
contain any sensitive data

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:42.070
either.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.870
Now, one final check.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.630
Let's see one of the files
in our sensitive bucket.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.300
And as expected,
this contains emails,

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.250
which is one of our
sensitive data parameters

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.240
that we had set.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.590
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So that's it.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.020
Dinner Winner has their
application automated

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.810
and can return to their
gamified recipe submission

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.003
with confidence.

00:04:00.003 --> 00:04:02.170
JENNY BROWN: Here's to the
pursuit of deliciousness.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:04.503
Check out the article linked
in the description for more

00:04:04.503 --> 00:04:05.545
step-by-step information.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.420
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: If
you're hungry for more,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.880
be sure to like, subscribe,
and catch us next time

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.550
for another episode of
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

